/* Overrides pico_standard_link/script_include/default_text_excludes.incl.
 *
 * This file is the safety mechanism for the PSRAM-execution scheme -- see the
 * long note in pico_flash_region.ld next to it. Read that first.
 *
 * HOW THIS WORKS: whatever is EXCLUDED from the flash (here: PSRAM) .text
 * section is picked up by the `*(.text*)` line in section_default_data.incl's
 * `.data` section, which lives in RAM. So naming an object here moves its code
 * into SRAM. This is the SDK's own mechanism -- the same one that puts
 * __not_in_flash_func code in RAM for ordinary flash builds -- not a trick.
 *
 * WHY THESE OBJECTS: board_init() does
 *
 *     vreg_set_voltage() -> sleep_ms() -> set_sys_clock_khz(250 MHz)
 *     -> clock_configure(clk_peri) -> psram_configure_params()
 *     -> psram_reinitialize()
 *
 * and the QMI M1 timing for PSRAM is STALE from set_sys_clock_khz() until
 * psram_reinitialize() completes. Under this scheme the CPU fetches its own
 * instructions through that same mis-timed window, so anything running in the
 * gap must already be in SRAM or the fetch itself is what fails. The list is
 * therefore "everything reachable between the re-clock and the re-time",
 * deliberately erring wide:
 *
 *   psram.c      psram_reinitialize / psram_initialize_internal / params
 *   flash.c      flash_start_xip / flash_exit_xip / flash_devinfo_*  (
 *                psram_reinitialize() reaches the QMI *through* flash_start_xip)
 *   xip_cache.c  cache maintenance around the re-time
 *   clocks.c     set_sys_clock_khz / clock_configure -- opens the window
 *   pll.c        reached by set_sys_clock_khz to retune the PLL
 *   vreg.c       the 1.25 V step immediately before
 *   timer.c      sleep_ms() is called inside the sequence
 *   board.c      the ORCHESTRATOR. Easy to forget: board_init() is itself
 *                executing across the whole gap, so if it stays in PSRAM the
 *                return from psram_reinitialize() has nowhere valid to go back
 *                to. It is in libfreewili2_bsp.a, hence the archive:member form.
 *
 * Note that objects named here cost SRAM, which is the resource this whole
 * scheme exists to save -- so the list should stay tight. It currently measures
 * ~16-18 KB, which is a striking match for the extra SRAM a known-good
 * production build copies over ours (~28.6 KB there vs our .data at 12.4 KB).
 * That is corroboration that the same approach was taken, not proof.
 *
 * The first four entries are the SDK's original list and MUST be kept -- libgcc
 * and libc memcpy/memset in particular are on the hot audio path.
 *
 * SECOND GROUP -- THE AUDIO PATH, kept in SRAM for LATENCY, not correctness.
 *
 * The DSP inner loops have a 5.2 ms capture deadline (invariant 15: audio runs
 * on core 1). Correctness does not require them in SRAM -- XIP-from-PSRAM
 * demonstrably runs the app -- but there is no reason to spend the deadline
 * budget on cache misses when SRAM is now the resource we have spare.
 *
 * This set was not guessed. The reference image copies 28,596 bytes to SRAM
 * where a PSRAM-timing-only build copies 6,252; the 22,344-byte difference is
 * matched to within 600 bytes by exactly these five objects:
 *
 *     fx 13,792 + vox 1,462 + synth 2,568 + drums 2,404 + keys 1,518 = 21,744
 *
 * viz.c (4,438) is deliberately NOT here: adding it overshoots his figure, and
 * the visualiser's FFT runs at display rate (~12 Hz), not on the audio deadline.
 * main.c (47,570) stays in PSRAM too -- it is UI code, and moving it would give
 * back most of the SRAM this scheme exists to reclaim. The per-sample work it
 * drives lives in the objects above, so the hot path is covered.
 *
 * THIRD GROUP -- THE DIAGNOSTICS, and this one was found the hard way.
 *
 * `board_init_clk()` calls DIAG() -> SEGGER_RTT_printf() BETWEEN the clock
 * change and psram_reinitialize(), i.e. INSIDE the mis-timed window. Leaving
 * the RTT implementation in PSRAM means the first thing that window does is
 * fetch an instruction through itself. On hardware that presented as: progress
 * bar fills (the loader staged and jumped fine), then a black screen with no
 * boot-trace markers and no recovery -- dead before the display came up.
 *
 * It hid because the direct callee is a long-branch VENEER at 0x20006cb8, which
 * IS in SRAM; it just trampolines to 0x11014cad. Auditing "what does board_init
 * call" therefore showed all-SRAM and looked fine. `panic` is here for the same
 * reason -- it is what a failed assertion inside that window would try to reach.
 *
 * tools/check_psram_xip.py now walks the call graph transitively THROUGH veneers
 * from board_init, so this class of miss is mechanical rather than remembered.
 * Add objects here; do not rely on reasoning about them.
 */
*(EXCLUDE_FILE(
    *libgcc.a: *libc.a: *lib*_a-mem*.o *libm.a:
    *psram.c.obj *flash.c.obj *xip_cache.c.obj
    *clocks.c.obj *pll.c.obj *vreg.c.obj *timer.c.obj
    *libfreewili2_bsp.a:board.c.obj
    *fx.c.obj *vox.c.obj *synth.c.obj *drums.c.obj *keys.c.obj
    *SEGGER_RTT.c.obj *SEGGER_RTT_printf.c.obj *panic.c.obj
    *sd_entry.c.obj
  ) .text*)
